# Session end

<!-- Render into the repo (e.g. docs/sessions/end.md) and link it from
     AGENTS.md "At session end". Five blocks. Each block is done or
     recorded — there is no third state. -->

## 1. The gate

```bash
{{CHECK_COMMAND}}
```

Green, or the failure is recorded with the exact next action. A skipped
relevant check is named and explained.

## 2. Leave it fast

Compare this session's timings with the recorded bounds in
`{{PERFORMANCE_DOC}}`. Two warm runs above a bound means fix the cause or
log a papercut. Never raise the recorded bound to make the run pass.

## 3. Leave it startable

- `{{READINESS_COMMAND}}` passes from a clean shell.
- Every row you touched names its exact next action.
- No stray processes, servers, ports, or temporary files left behind.

## 4. Leave the map true

- Routers list what exists — nothing missing, nothing dead.
- One owner per fact. A fact repeated in two docs is a bug.
- Plain sentences. Short, active, one idea each.

## 5. Eliminate this session's papercuts

Append new friction to `{{PAPERCUTS_LOG}}` while you work. Entries are
stateless: no status, no owner.

**No entry you added may survive this session as a raw log entry.** Give
each one an exit before you stop:

- **Trivial and safe** — ≤15 minutes, one surface, no schema, money, or
  auth: fix it now and close the entry.
- **Not trivial** — write a feature row, or a blocked row that names its
  exact gate. Then close the entry.

Also attempt at least one pre-existing entry that touches the surfaces you
worked on. A future run must never hit a papercut this session could have
removed.

Session start and session end are the only cleanup mechanisms. There is no
periodic sweep, so these five blocks must be enough on their own.
